      "id": "wvgo50q",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Nature of the Revolt"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Revolt of 1857 was a major turning point in modern Indian history, signifying the first widespread armed uprising against British rule.",
        "Multiple factors contributed to the uprising, including political annexation doctrines, severe economic exploitation, socio-religious interference, and military grievances.",
        "The immediate trigger was the introduction of grease-lubricated cartridges for the Enfield rifle, offending Hindu and Muslim sepoys.",
        "Major centers of the revolt included Delhi, Kanpur, Lucknow, Jhansi, and Gwalior, led by figures like Bahadur Shah Zafar, Rani Lakshmibai, Nana Saheb, and Begum Hazrat Mahal.",
        "The revolt led directly to the end of British East India Company rule and the passage of the Government of India Act 1858, transferring power to the British Crown."
      ],
      "id": "czdz7bm"
    },
